    <string name="game_hint_explanation_last_digit">%1$s is the only empty cell in %2$s, so it must be %3$d.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_naked_single">%1$s has only one possible candidate: %2$d.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_hidden_single">In %2$s, candidate %1$s can only go in %3$s.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_naked_subset">In %1$s, %2$s contain only %3$s. Those digits can be removed from the other cells.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_hidden_subset">In %1$s, %2$s can only occur in %3$s. Remove the other candidates from those cells.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_pointing">In %2$s, candidate %1$s is confined to %3$s. Remove it from the rest of that line.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_claiming">In %2$s, candidate %1$s is confined to %3$s. Remove it from the rest of that box.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_fish">Candidate %1$s is confined to %3$s across the base houses %2$s. It can be removed from the other cells in those cover houses.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_single_digit_chain">%1$s links candidate %2$s through %3$s. The alternating strong and weak inferences make the target candidate impossible.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_multi_digit_chain">%1$s uses the linked cells %2$s and candidates %3$s. Either end of the inference forces the same elimination.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_wxyz_wing">Cells %2$s form a four-cell wing using only %3$s; the two wing groups share only %1$d. If a target were %1$d, the pivot values would leave too few distinct digits for the wings, so that candidate can be removed.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_aic">%1$s alternates strong and weak links through candidates %3$s in %2$s. At least one endpoint must be true, so candidates that conflict with both endpoints can be removed.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_nishio_forcing">Assuming %1$s is true leads to the contradiction shown by the %2$s graph. The assumption is false.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_verity_forcing">%1$s follows every possible branch from %2$s. Every branch reaches the same conclusion shown on the board.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_forcing_region_premise">candidate %1$d in %2$s</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_remote_pair">The pair %2$s alternates through %1$s. A cell that sees both opposite ends cannot contain either digit.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_chute_remote_pair">The chute remote-pair structure in %1$s locks the pair %2$s across its bands or stacks, producing the shown elimination.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_coloring_type_1">The conjugate chain for candidate %1$s colors %2$s in two alternating colors. The target sees both colors, so that candidate can be removed.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_coloring_type_2">The conjugate chain for candidate %1$s colors %2$s in two alternating colors. Two cells of one color see each other, so that color is false.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_empty_rectangle">Candidate %1$s forms an empty rectangle with the conjugate link through %2$s. Both possibilities eliminate the target candidate.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_bug_plus_one">Every other unsolved cell is bivalue. To avoid a BUG deadly pattern, %1$s must be %2$d.</string>
    <string name="game_hint_explanation_unique_rectangle">%1$s uses the uniqueness pattern in %2$s with candidates %3$s. Keeping the target candidate would create a deadly rectangle with two solutions.</string>
